#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db6c711e-a164-50b5-bfec-66eb3cd983c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable only for certain initial conditions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359474} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ffea3e-b54a-599a-a784-6d10da09dad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Unstable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359475} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1a3bdf-2e9a-55ce-80ff-de9408d3d51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359476} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ca0e9b-d61e-5c9e-a3e6-56e9bc0fbccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Stable but not asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359477} {:answer/id #uuid "22ff3695-5149-575b-9a4a-78208dae0586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable only for nonlinear systems." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0ca0e9b-d61e-5c9e-a3e6-56e9bc0fbccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Stable but not asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359477} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac1cde9-39f2-5443-a24a-e79a254128b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2b4530a-6c6c-5e8b-9d00-bf27eb10da96" :question/math-academy-id "q-335465" :question/problem "A small perturbation away from an equilibrium causes trajectories to remain in a bounded region around the equilibrium, sometimes oscillating or circling, but they do not converge to the equilibrium as time increases. How should the equilibrium be classified?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Stable but not asymptotically stable.\""}]}
